13.m:
These best of twice-born ones exist with a single body;
they are worthy of worship by the dwellers of the earth, dwelling in the heavenly world;
the sons of Brahmā, Marīci and others, who have attained the Great [realm], are worthy of worship by them.

16.m:
Then Vidyut and Suvidyut, resorting to yoga, O fair one,
resorted to the fever of intense heat by the application of the wind's function;
by the yoga of creative illusion they performed the entire role of the world-guardians.

25.m:
You have the nature of fire in the tree and likewise in the stone;
you have the nature of sattva and are likewise in the waters;
the Lord, the Great Lord, has the nature of light:
O Hara, protect us who are afflicted by the host of Daityas!

All the oceans, the principal mountain ranges,
and the river-bearing streams on every side are in the form of your matted hair;
the moon is this supreme knowledge situated within you;
people with evil vision, O god, do not see this.

You are Nārāyaṇa, the origin of the worlds;
likewise you are Bhava, and you yourself are the four-faced one;
by the divisions of sattva and the other qualities, through the division of the fires,
and through the division of yugas and the rest, you abide as threefold.

The gods said:
“O lord of the gods, give us a commander for the slaying of the Daityas;
this alone will bring welfare to the gods headed by Brahmā.”

27.m:
Desire, anger, likewise greed, pride, and delusion is the fifth;
envy they call the sixth, and malice the seventh,
and jealousy is to be known as the eighth: these are the eight Mothers.

Delusion is the self-born Kaumārī, and envy they know as born of Indra;
the goddess bearing the staff of Yama is malice itself,
and jealousy is named Vārāhī: thus these are proclaimed.
